  <h1>What to Do After Age Discrimination</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Document immediately and thoroughly</h2>
  <p>Write a detailed account of every relevant incident: dates, times, locations, who was present, exact words used. Note any age-related comments by supervisors or decision-makers, even if they seem like jokes. Save performance reviews (especially recent positive ones that undercut any performance-based justification), emails, and HR communications. If you were replaced, try to learn the age of your replacement.</p>
  <h2>2. Preserve comparator evidence</h2>
  <p>ADEA claims depend heavily on comparison to younger workers who were treated more favorably. Note which colleagues (with similar roles, tenure, or performance) were retained while you were terminated or passed over. Their approximate ages matter. Courts routinely look at whether younger, comparable employees received different treatment.</p>
  <h2>3. File an EEOC charge &mdash; watch the deadline</h2>
  <p>For private employers, you must file an EEOC charge within <strong>180 days</strong> of the discriminatory act (or <strong>300 days</strong> in states with a fair employment practices agency &mdash; which includes most major states). This is an absolute deadline. Missing it usually means losing your federal ADEA claim. File at eeoc.gov or at your nearest EEOC office.</p>
  <h2>4. Consider state law claims</h2>
  <p>Many states have broader age discrimination protections: lower employee thresholds (California&rsquo;s FEHA covers employers with 5+ employees), no willfulness requirement for doubled damages, and longer statutes of limitations. State claims can be filed concurrently with or after the EEOC process.</p>
  <h2>5. Consult an employment attorney early</h2>
  <p>ADEA cases are won and lost on evidence gathered early. An attorney can help you preserve documents, identify the right parties (sometimes a private equity owner or parent company is a joint employer), and navigate the EEOC process. Most employment attorneys take ADEA cases on contingency &mdash; no fee unless you win.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-adea-damages-work">how ADEA damages work</a> and <a href="/guides/common-adea-misconceptions">common ADEA misconceptions</a>.</p>
